{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The persistent workspace and its authoritative inputs are the continuing truth surface: their current material state determines what work has occurred. An actor's context is a bounded, revisable and disposable projection, never another truth surface. Work state, workspace and closed results carry continuity without a further carrier.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1478,
          "quote": "An account is\nnever a second truth surface, and no later activation needs a prior\nactor's context: its intake reconstructs the governing relations without\nit",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F5.%20Work%20state%20and%20restart%2FThe%20workspace%20and%20an%20actor%27s%20account",
          "start_line": 1475
        }
      ],
      "key": "workspace_and_actor_account",
      "label": "Workspace truth and actor account"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Proportionality relates semantic ambiguity removed to effective reasoning complexity introduced. Complexity includes independent concepts and authority surfaces, cross-document joins, exceptions, branches, states, transitions, reconciled duplicate representations and decision-relevant detail; it is not line count, artifact count or context-window size. Rival authority, failure-classification uncertainty and evidence uncertainty count only insofar as they leave materially different semantic, authority, outcome or acceptance interpretations, not as additional benefits.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1534,
          "quote": "Proportionality is the relation between semantic ambiguity removed and\neffective reasoning complexity introduced.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F6.%20Cost%20and%20proportionality",
          "start_line": 1530
        }
      ],
      "key": "proportionality",
      "label": "Proportionality"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "A cost claim is the governed question."
      ],
      "definition": "A selected cost claim has an activation-declared allowance and reported spend measured under its selected execution binding or explicitly unmeasured. Material startup, framework processing, external computation, transport and assurance costs are separate. Supported input dimensions and workloads precede qualification; transferred-context cost includes every delegated actor, not just the coordinator. Report volume differs from model traffic.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1592,
          "quote": "Cost is selected, not universal. Where a cost claim is the governed\nquestion, the activation declares its allowance and reports its spend,\nmeasured under the selected execution binding or recorded as unmeasured",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F6.%20Cost%20and%20proportionality",
          "start_line": 1589
        }
      ],
      "key": "selected_cost_claim",
      "label": "Selected cost claim"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A resource limit bounds activation consumption of context, attention, wall time, spend, human effort, communication or machine resources. The subsection owns the generic declaration, measurement and observance law, not a numerical value. The selected execution binding sets values and measurable units and boundaries; SPEC_METHOD §11 supplies the referenced build-tenant binding.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1643,
          "quote": "A resource limit bounds a resource that an activation consumes: context,\nattention, wall time, spend, human effort, communication or a machine\nresource (P0). This subsection owns the law for every such limit. It sets\nno value",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F6.%20Cost%20and%20proportionality%2FResource%20limits",
          "start_line": 1640
        }
      ],
      "key": "resource_limit",
      "label": "Resource limit"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A closed result identifies its frame, activation, subject, basis, evaluation, actor, evidence, verdict, residual uncertainty and invalidation conditions. It supports handoff without private working context while preserving provenance, validity and independence conditions. A result is affected by a change when its dependencies D*(t) meet impact cover I(Δ). Invalidation withdraws direct reliance, not necessarily its evidence and not necessarily the truth of its claim.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1697,
          "quote": "A closed result identifies the frame and its activation, the subject,\nthe basis, the evaluation, the actor, the evidence, the verdict, the residual\nuncertainty and the invalidation conditions",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F7.%20Closed%20result%20and%20reuse",
          "start_line": 1694
        }
      ],
      "key": "closed_result",
      "label": "Closed result"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The three invalidator kinds are material input change, changed bound basis coordinate, and occurrence of a revising observation declared by the result or judgment. Material input includes newly acquired or changed admissible evidence that could change the result's value. Changed source, task or actor conditions count as basis changes where bound, otherwise material input changes; a role change matters when it changes a required actor condition.",
      "exceptions": [
        "Role, conversation or resumption change that changes none of these invalidates nothing."
      ],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1732,
          "quote": "- a changed basis: a change to any coordinate of the basis the result\n  binds (FOUNDATIONS §2, Basis);\n- a declared revising observation: an observation that the result or\n  judgment itself names as one that would revise it, once it occurs.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F7.%20Closed%20result%20and%20reuse",
          "start_line": 1729
        }
      ],
      "key": "result_invalidator",
      "label": "Closed-result invalidator"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Transition evidence is a consumer-represented read model identifying exact candidate, affected relations, changed realisation paths, added or removed authority paths, retained and open seams, focused proof, required integration proof and explicit non-changes. If an assessor was activated it includes that assessor's recommended disposition; once the decision owner decides, the owner's disposition appears beside it.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1770,
          "quote": "Transition evidence is a read\nmodel; it does not become specification, design or admission authority",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F7.%20Closed%20result%20and%20reuse%2FTransition%20evidence",
          "start_line": 1768
        }
      ],
      "key": "transition_evidence",
      "label": "Transition evidence"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The source-disposition ledger reports predecessor conservation, new successor material, ownership and proposed changes. It reports UP-020 and six subsections conserved across §§1,3,5,7, with generic work state, uncertain-effect reconciliation and non-idempotence new; workspace/account, family tables, transitions, configuration, effects, derived-surface staleness, refinement additions and resource-limit law new. It routes UP-018 to DESIGN_MODULE §7 and general reconstruction to Intake; probabilistic work boundary to FOUNDATIONS; RF engagement across §§1–4,7; AC-010/011 to AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS with §2 applying them with RF-002/003/004/014/017; RF-012/AC-007 and result conjunction to FOUNDATIONS §4; admission provenance to TICKETS with new core mappings; ambiguity outcomes and hard stops to §§3–4; ODD graph functions to generic typed refinement while ODD 11.8 remains profile law; UP-017 to DESIGN_MODULE §5 and local seam rules; UP-021 and standing structural qualification to §4; UP-014 to §6 with delivery priority in SPEC_METHOD §8, and UP-005/019 to DESIGN_MODULE; closed-result return and generic reuse to §7 and ticket continuity to TICKETS §7. These are source-authored disposition reports, not findings of conservation by this interpretation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1788,
          "quote": "a table decides nothing and names the rule that states each consequence.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ACTIVATION.md#ACTIVATION%2F8.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line": 1788
        }
      ],
      "key": "activation_source_disposition_ledger",
      "label": "ACTIVATION predecessor-disposition ledger"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "ASSURANCE is the tier-2 owner of assurance and testing law: verification layers, test taxonomy and authority, fixture portability, scenarios, installed proof, significant paths, testing frames, combination coverage, path erasure, evidence composition, subject acquisition, localisation and interface integration. It consumes tier-1 FOUNDATIONS, REFERENCE_FRAME, EVALUATION and ACTIVATION without adding tier-1 definitions. SPEC_METHOD supplies chain/change classes; PRODUCT_DEFINITION supplies project bindings. Design-profile module proof and TICKETS test-case authority retain their scopes. EXECUTIVE_BASELINE maps roles at tier 3 without being a dependency. The two predecessor anchor conventions are provenance only; the standalone successor claim says received rows are restated without requiring the predecessor cut.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 20,
          "quote": "reuse); adds no definition to tier 1.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE",
          "start_line": 20
        }
      ],
      "key": "assurance_member",
      "label": "ASSURANCE member"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Requirements preserve invariant truth and ask what must be true; design preserves structural choice and asks how that truth is realised; scenarios preserve operational meaning and ask whether the realised system can do the claimed thing. Scenarios are concrete end-to-end use cases at the product-owner layer, validating intent through realised behaviour.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 47,
          "quote": "| Requirements | invariant truth | what must be true? |\n| Design | structural choice | how is that truth realised? |\n| Scenarios | operational meaning | can the realised system actually do the claimed thing? |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE%2F1.%20Verification%20layers",
          "start_line": 45
        }
      ],
      "key": "verification_layers",
      "label": "Distinct verification layers"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "The relevant instance premises apply; commitments alone do not supply them."
      ],
      "definition": "F14 accounts for assurance §§1–16 and identifies evidence law, its stated foundation premises and added domain choices. Instance premises are executable/observable source and runnable product forms; modules, interfaces and owners composed along causal paths; user outcomes through supported surfaces; external workers, transports or fixtures; and optionally a design profile governing module ownership. P0 is recovered; P1, P2, P3, P4a and P6 are independent where cited; P5 derives from P0/P6 with the open-world premise; P4b derives from P0/P2 with non-reacquirable step-produced facts. §17 is disposition recording, not decision authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 84,
          "quote": "Its instance premises, which the commitments\ndo not supply, are named in the rows as follows: runnable product, a\nproduct's realisation can be executed or observed, in a source form and in\na deployed, installed or otherwise runnable form, to produce evidence;",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE%2F1.%20Verification%20layers%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20assurance%20and%20testing",
          "start_line": 81
        }
      ],
      "key": "assurance_foundation_family",
      "label": "Assurance and testing foundation family"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Within urn:stdo:bounded-context:product-assurance under the selected complete STDO basis, proof authority has two primary sources: design/module conformance and constitutional user/Product acceptance. Conformance derives from governing design, module ownership, declared carrier inventory or equivalent, structural diagrams where present and boundary-local fail-closed law; unit tests belong here, and integration, negative or fail-closed tests may belong here when targeting a designed boundary. Acceptance derives from requirements, acceptance criteria, scenarios/use cases and Product release/qualification claims. Execution breadth, speed and live/deterministic mode do not determine authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 159,
          "quote": "Authority source is not execution breadth. A test may be small or broad,\nfast or slow, deterministic or live, and still derives from one of two\nprimary authorities (P6, P1):\n\n1. design or module conformance tests; and\n2. UAT, or acceptance, tests.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE%2F2.%20Testing%20strategy%20taxonomy",
          "start_line": 154
        }
      ],
      "key": "test_authority_taxonomy",
      "label": "Product test-authority taxonomy"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Sandbox UAT exercises a deployed, installed or otherwise runnable composed Product through declared application, public, runtime or control surfaces, driven by a requirement-sourced scenario or acceptance case. Harnessed mode uses deterministic, fake, recorded or injected external workers/results and proves exercised composition, orchestration, control, projection, archive and scenario wiring. Live mode crosses the real configured external worker/tool/agent/service/transport and returns evidence through declared result, evaluation, provenance and projection surfaces.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 194,
          "quote": "- harnessed sandbox UAT exercises the full composed product path with a\n  deterministic, fake, recorded or injected worker or result surface; and\n- live sandbox UAT exercises the full composed product path with a real\n  configured worker, tool, agent, service or other external probabilistic\n  transport where the product depends on that live boundary.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE%2F2.%20Testing%20strategy%20taxonomy",
          "start_line": 190
        }
      ],
      "key": "sandbox_uat",
      "label": "Harnessed and live sandbox UAT"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Fixture portability concerns reproducibility of required closure lanes from a declared source boundary and explicit treatment of external project evidence. A missing fixture is a governed diagnostic naming fixture authority and lane, not a Product semantic failure.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 239,
          "quote": "A missing external fixture fails with a governed diagnostic that names the\nfixture authority and the lane; it never masquerades as a product semantic\nfailure",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE%2F2.%20Testing%20strategy%20taxonomy%2FFixture%20and%20proof%20portability",
          "start_line": 237
        }
      ],
      "key": "fixture_portability",
      "label": "Fixture and proof portability"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Written, traceable test-case authority includes appropriate scenario bundles, ordered sequences, negative/control cases or other explicit evidence cases. Each expected assertion carries its owning obligation, interpretation, assumptions, derivation, shared dependencies and whether its predicate is the obligation itself, a necessary condition or part of a sufficient conjunction.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 251,
          "quote": "Written authority includes the assertion\nprovenance of each expected result: the owning obligation, its\ninterpretation, assumptions, derivation and shared dependencies, and\nwhether the asserted predicate is the obligation, a necessary condition of\nit or part of a sufficient conjunction",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE%2F3.%20Test%20authority",
          "start_line": 247
        }
      ],
      "key": "written_test_case_authority",
      "label": "Written test-case authority"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A scenario bundle exposes the feature/use-case boundary, coherent ordered test sequence and expected outcomes at each significant step. Declared scenarios pressure intent and design to name operational boundary, sequence, actors, controls and outcomes, and are described as the best source for identifying significant code/control paths.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 271,
          "quote": "A scenario bundle shows the feature or use-case boundary being exercised,\nthe test-case sequence that proves it, and the outcomes expected at each\nsignificant step. It is the layer that makes operational meaning\ninspectable",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE%2F4.%20Scenario%20bundle",
          "start_line": 268
        }
      ],
      "key": "scenario_bundle",
      "label": "Scenario bundle"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "The system has an installable or runnable development form."
      ],
      "definition": "Installed-development proof is the decisive lane against an installed development version treated as an independent project or external target. Its recommended form uses a fresh sandbox or equivalent isolation, an installed/built development artifact and the real Product's declared entry, control and runtime surfaces.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 285,
          "quote": "Where a system has an installable or runnable development form, the\ndecisive proving lane runs against an installed development version as if\nit were an independent project or an externalised target, not only against\nthe source tree in place",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE%2F5.%20Installed%20dev%20proof",
          "start_line": 282
        }
      ],
      "key": "installed_dev_proof",
      "label": "Installed development proof"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Significant path coverage intentionally names and proves meaningful paths for the claimed behaviour, not every branch in every file. Usual paths, where relevant, are intended success; fail-closed/rejection; boundary/control; integration/dispatch/transport; and recovery/retry/replay.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 299,
          "quote": "Exhaustive proof does not mean every possible branch in every file; it\nmeans the project names the meaningful paths and proves them\nintentionally. The significant paths usually include, where relevant:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE%2F6.%20Significant%20path%20coverage",
          "start_line": 297
        }
      ],
      "key": "significant_path_coverage",
      "label": "Significant path coverage"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A testing frame executes or observes a declared subject to discriminate one exact claim using an explicit oracle, evidence population, path boundary and falsification condition. A file, suite, runner, fixture, scenario label, percentage or actor is not a frame merely by existing; counts, duration and framework category cannot enlarge its exercised claim.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 319,
          "quote": "A testing frame is a frame (REFERENCE_FRAME §1) whose evaluation executes\nor observes a declared subject to discriminate one exact claim through an\nexplicit oracle, evidence population, path boundary and falsification\ncondition.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE%2F7.%20Product%20testing%20frame%20set%2FTerms",
          "start_line": 316
        }
      ],
      "key": "testing_frame",
      "label": "Testing frame"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The authoritative Product path is the declared ordinary causal composition from supported user/public entry through exact selected interfaces, owners, state transitions, effects, persistence and recovery boundaries to the required outcome projection. Material combinatorial complexity is the claim-relative population of independent dimensions and interactions able to change outcomes, violate invariants, select another authority path or expose failure. Unit dimensions include module public-contract laws, operators, calculation regimes, states, transitions, boundary classes and failures; cross-participant, Product, persistence, configuration and user-role combinations belong at integration, end-to-end or user-acceptance boundaries.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 329,
          "quote": "An authoritative Product path is the declared ordinary causal composition\nfrom a supported user or public entry, through the exact selected\ninterfaces, owners, state transitions, effects, persistence and recovery\nboundaries, to the outcome projection the Product claim requires.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE%2F7.%20Product%20testing%20frame%20set%2FTerms",
          "start_line": 326
        }
      ],
      "key": "authoritative_product_path",
      "label": "Authoritative Product path and its material combinations"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Governing frame configuration or admitted work authority selects testing frames for a Product promotion, closure or release-assurance claim."
      ],
      "definition": "The ordinary claim-relative testing set contains user acceptance for supported user outcome, end to end for complete runnable causal realisation, integration for one material composition boundary and interaction population, and unit for one module-owned law. It adds no role, stage, authority hierarchy or testing hierarchy. Claims, subjects, paths, populations, oracles and falsification boundaries select frames, not runners. Placement makes the set and §§8–13 available without adoption or activation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 355,
          "quote": "Placement in this member makes the\nset, and the law of §8 to §13 that serves it, available; it adopts nothing,\nand each activation is decided by ACTIVATION §1 (Applicability).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE%2F7.%20Product%20testing%20frame%20set%2FThe%20set",
          "start_line": 351
        }
      ],
      "key": "product_testing_frame_set",
      "label": "Claim-relative Product testing frame set"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "User acceptance asks whether a declared user with admitted capability/information can obtain the exact Product-defined outcome through supported ordinary surfaces. Its subject/basis binds exact runnable Product, current Product/requirement/UX/identity/policy/acceptance authority, user role, environment and harnessed/live mode. Its manifold covers goal, entries, permissions, inputs, configuration, dependencies/substitutions, visible state/output, refusal, recovery and acceptance interval. Coordinates distinguish Product/artifact/install, role/capability, configuration, dependency realisation/mode, data/workspace/invocation/time/outcome/scenario. Invariants include applicable outcomes, interface/identity/UX/safety/persistence/release law and referenced RF/EVALUATION laws. Evidence comprises supported requests/responses, projections, claimed durable effects, refusals, recovery, scenarios and exact subject/dependency identity. Capability must reproduce the user's access and distinguish hidden assistance. Result is one exact user-outcome core result with witnessed paths, counterexamples, residual user populations and invalidators.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 412,
          "quote": "Can a declared user, with the user's admitted capability and information, obtain the exact Product-defined outcome through the supported ordinary Product surface?",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE%2F7.%20Product%20testing%20frame%20set%2FUser-acceptance%20frame",
          "start_line": 412
        }
      ],
      "key": "user_acceptance_frame",
      "label": "User-acceptance frame"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "End to end asks whether the exact runnable Product realises its outcome across every material interface, owner, state transition, effect, persistence, continuation, recovery and projection boundary. Basis binds composition design, supported entry, selected owners/dependencies, ordinary configuration and Product claim. Manifold includes all causal producers/consumers, contracts, authority transitions, state/events/processes, retry/replay/recovery and rivals. Coordinates include artifact/installed tree, entry/operation, definition/owner, paths/edges, process/event prefix/state, configuration/dependency/result/replay/scenario. Invariants cover composition, exact subject/congruence, required path singularity and lifecycle/authority/persistence/release. Evidence includes ordinary invocation, semantic path/authority edges, owner receipts, durable events, transitions, outputs, claimed reconstruction, artifact identity and negative paths. Capability observes the complete path unchanged and reproduces material states/failures. Result binds exact end-to-end claim, exercised edges, unexercised paths, counterexamples and residual combinations.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 444,
          "quote": "including every material interface, owner, state transition, effect, persistence, continuation, recovery and projection boundary?",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ASSURANCE.md#ASSURANCE%2F7.%20Product%20testing%20frame%20set%2FEnd-to-end%20frame",
          "start_line": 444
        }
      ],
      "key": "end_to_end_frame",
      "label": "End-to-end frame"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Integration evaluates actual participants' joint contract at one declared material composition boundary across result-changing state, authority, configuration, timing, failure and recovery combinations. Basis binds exact declaration, actual implementations or expressly admitted substitutes, governing Product/requirement/design law and population. Manifold includes both sides, carriers, pre/postconditions, ownership, ordering, state, retry/recovery, translation and rivals. Coordinates identify participants/versions, interface/carrier revisions, state/configuration/dependency, case/input/output/failure/evidence. Invariants include composition law, complete participant admission, applicable atomicity/reconstruction and exact assurance boundary. Evidence comprises actual exchanges, admitted carriers, transitions, verdicts, failures/retries/recovery, combinations and identities. Capability exercises the real seam, detects substitution and varies every material interaction dimension. Result binds exact integration claim, covered combinations, counterexamples, omissions and invalidators.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 467,
          "quote": "Do the actual participants at one declared material composition boundary satisfy their joint contract across the combinations of state, authority, configuration, timing, failure and recovery that can change the boundary result?",
